267 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Design Rationale)/Pg 13 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

268 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Research: Nothing is ever born from a vacuum, so the team\220s brainstorming process included researching previous designs both related to the competition and unrelated components which may have proved useful, comparing them to the resources freely available to the team, and then analyzing the feasibility of each component. The thruster mounts and layout were a particularly tricky piece of the puzzle to research, requiring a knowledge of fluid dynamics to effectively design.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

269 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Identifying problems and constraints: The thruster mounts were originally designed to be at the four corners of the pressure hull as well as at the cross-sections, completely surrounding the pressure hull. This mathematically made sense and would have been very maneuverable. However, this was unfeasible because that would severely limit the team\220s access to the front and back caps.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

270 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Finding solutions: The thruster mounts were redesigned to be stacked horizontally on each other to prevent thruster wash and to allow for six degrees of freedom.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

271 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Prototype and Testing: The original stacked design had the screws holding the T-200s hidden within the mounts, but that was quickly adjusted for the sake of maintenanceability. The trade-off was that the T-200s would be more susceptible to vibrations, however this did not seem like something that would be a major issue.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

272 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(ROSIE\220s system design approach focused on subsystem integration and iteration, meaning, all the subsystems worked together at the same time to organically develop the entire system while emphasizing the team members individual passions for different aspects of the project.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

273 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Subsystems: Before assigning team members to their subsystems they were given the chance to try all different systems pertaining to their career emphasis. Mechanical engineering majors were spread out amongst the ROV Chassis, Float Chassis, and Manipulator teams. Electrical engineering majors were spread out to the ROV Controls and Electronics, Float Controls and Electronics, and GUI teams. All other majors got to choose where they went.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

274 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Communication: There were weekly meetings and check-ins to ensure that every subsystem was on the same page in terms of design. As designs would develop, the leads would contact each other and discuss the feasibility of the changes.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

275 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(CAD software like Solidworks was invaluable for evaluating how components fit together and for designing the parts alongside each other. The files were organized using a spreadsheet and numbering system that allowed the mechanical team to organize their CAD files per subsystem and easily communicate with each other.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

