284 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(filament color of the ROV because it has the highest strength of standard color blends.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

285 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(When submitted for printing, SMS parts were assigned to one of two continuously-refined setting groups: air test or water test and a PDF was sent to the printer for each component \(see Figure X\). Air test prints used 15% infill and 2-3 walls, allowing for maximum filament saving and quick initial fit tests. Water tests used 35-50% infill and 5 walls and shells, allowing for optimized strength and minimized mass absorption underwater.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

286 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(That being said, the 3D printed parts do absorb water and this was used to add ballast to the system and prevent the legs from swinging up towards the surface of the water.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

287 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(The overall vehicle size was kept as compact as possible to improve maneuverability and reduce drag underwater while still leaving enough room for all major subsystems. Thrusters were spread out to avoid thruster wash and to increase stability in currents. Weight was also considered throughout the design process, so reinforcement was only added where necessary instead of overbuilding the structure. Throughout development, the team compared different materials and design options based on strength, weight, manufacturability, cost, and how well each component integrated with the rest of the vehicle.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

288 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(Big priorities in the design were: weight \(must be less than 18kg\) \205 for maximum bonus points and ease of transport and handling, low cost \205 being a first year team SMS had limited resources to bring the vision to life, and modularity \205 because setting a foundation of modularity sets the future of the company up for success. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

289 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(PETG was selected as SMS\220s primary filament for reasons documented in Table 2. PETG is accessible to beginner 3D printers, and has acceptable tensile strength, toughness, water resistance, and heat resistance for SMS\220s applications. PLA was used in some prototyping by was prone to warping \(especially if left in a car in the Central Valley heat\). TPU was selected for flexible grip designs for its ability to conform to the shape of objects grasped by the manipulator. Black was selected as the primary )/Pg 14 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

290 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(Table 2: Decision Matrix for filament types)/Pg 14 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

