<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Innovations)/Pg 15 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

295 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 125 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[1364 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

296 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 125 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[1365 0 R 0]/K[2]>>
endobj

297 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 125 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[1366 0 R 0]/K[3]>>
endobj

298 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Figure 8: Thruster Mount jig)/Pg 15 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

299 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(A significant paint point identified early in the season was the installation of T-nuts into the T-slot rail. One would think they would be easy to install, since they are made to be used together. However, the team quickly noticed the T-nuts sliding around in the rail channels and falling out and otherwise being an enormous pain to deal with. The solution was a set of thin jigs that slid into the channels and held the T-nuts in position so they could be easily screwed into. Two versions were created, the thruster mount jig \(Figure X\) which would be printed in sets of four, and the general jig \(Figure X\) which was used to mount the rotating manipulator and its motors.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

300 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Figure 7: General jig)/Pg 15 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

301 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Another innovation the team made was the harpoons. While harpoons in and of themselves are not revolutionary, they absolutely changed the game for SMS. The original purpose of the harpoons were simply to efficiently drive-by grab the basket stars, thereby reducing trips to the surface. However, they proved useful for a mulltiude of tasks such as replacing the eDNA sensor, carrying spools, picking up the lobster pot, etc. )/Pg 15 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

302 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Previous to making the jigs, the rough set-up time was about 2 hours to attach the thruster mounts and manipulator onto the chassis. After the jigs, maintenancing time for ROSIE was reduced to 30 minutes for removal and reattachment of the thruster mounts and the manipulator \(assuming the manipulator didn\220t need any other work done\).)/Pg 15 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

303 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Figure 9: Harpoon)/Pg 15 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

304 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(With curved fins that allow smooth grabbing and retention of the basket stars, a lip that prevents objects from accidental dropping, the harpoons proved just as versatile as the rotating manipulator. On the qualification run of ROSIE, the basket star, eDNA sensor, cap, and coral sample were all able to be brought to the surface simultaneously. An additional two harpoons were added to the back of the ROV that can be seen by the back camera so the pilots can back into samples that may move away from ROSIE in the current.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

305 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 125 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[1367 0 R 0]/K[11]>>
endobj

306 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Figure 10: All four harpoons mounted to the chassis)/Pg 15 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

307 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 125 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[1360 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
endobj

308 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(Vehicle Systems)/Pg 16 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

309 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(Design Evolution)/Pg 16 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

310 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(For the propulsion system, T-200 thrusters were selected for their compact size, reliability, and high thrust output for the cost. A layout of eight thrusters was selected to achieve all six degrees of freedom and maintain stability in a current.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

311 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 126 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[1368 0 R 0]/K[3]>>
endobj

312 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(Figure 11: Thruster Mount with T-200 propellers)/Pg 16 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

313 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(The manipulator rotates smoothly and easily with the help of plastic bushings. These were made of a plastic blend that offers low friction, self-kubrication, and moisture resistance.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

314 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(The plastic material won\220t rust like most metal bearings and was chosen over stainless steel or ceramic bearings that would have been more expensive. The bushings were also thinner than bearings, allowing for stronger/wider rotation shaft.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

315 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(torque. The rotating T-slot is a cantilever beam holding the weight of the motors, claw, and any collected object. Due to this we needed a way to support the T-slot while allowing it to spin, increasing the overall width. Furthermore, since the motors were in-line with each other, this greatly increased the overall length needed. Our second version of the rotation mechanism used gears to achieve rotation. In this design both the rotation and leadscrew motor are nearly side by side. Mounted to the back of the baseplate of the claw is the driven gear, this gear is concentric with the leadscrew, the leadscrew passes through this gear. A shaft on the driven gear then slots into a shaft mount with bearings. The rotation gear drives the driven gear ,turning the entire claw. The rotation gear was directly powered by the rotation motor. Since both motors are rigidly mounted side by side, the overall size was decreased and the issue of wires binding up was solved. However this design still had a few important issues. Most metal bearings will corrode underwater, ceramic and stainless steel bearings were expensive and hard to source. Furthermore bearings are somewhat thick, this thickness would require us to decide between a large shaft mount or a smaller/weaker drive gear shaft. The rotation motor was also too fast for our needs and didn't offer the desired torque. The weight of an object could also backdrive our motor. Our final rotation design functions very similarly to the second version except with a few changes to account for the issues with the previous design. The most major change was the addition of 2 4:1 planetary gearboxes for the rotation motor. This achieves a 16:1 ratio greatly increasing torque and reducing rotation speed. The gearbox also prevents backdriving of the rotaion motor. Plastic bushings were also used in place of bearings as they are thinner allowing room for a thicker shaft. Bushings are also longer allowing an increase to the length of the shaft and shaft mount increasing the claws stability. Both motor mounts were combined into one mount and the gearbox and shaft mount were also combined into one housing to help increase the compactness and reduce the number of parts to assemble/disassemble. Additionally, A ring shaped cap that is slightly larger in diameter than the shaft mounting hole is to the end of the shaft so that it cannot slide out of the shaft hole, while still allowing the shaft to rotate.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

316 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(The first version of the manipulator\220s rotation mechanism functioned by spinning the entire t-slot that the manipulator parts are mounted to. The second rotation motor would have been directly attached to one end of the t-slot so that it could directly rotate the t-slot. This idea was quickly abandoned as it appeared to have many issues. One major one is that since the leadscrew motor was mounted to the rotating t-slot, if the t-slot rotated too much in one direction the motor wires would bind up, preventing further rotation and potentially damaging the wires. The design was also not very rigid or stable. The rotation motor would need to turn the entire weight of the manipulator, increasing needed)/Pg 16 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

