326 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 127 0 R/E(ROSIE\220s propulsion design went through five distinct iterations, shown in the figures above. Each design prioritized obtaining 6 degrees of freedom to maximize our ability to approach and acquire mission props.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

327 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/BlockQuote/P 127 0 R/T(Version 0 was a foamboard mockup of an octagon shaped chassis, employing four vertical thrusters and two horizontal thrusters. Each foamboard panel represented an aluminum T-slot rail. SMS shifted away from this design because of the high density of the T-slot rail and the tolerancing complexity of the 32 required 45\260 angle cuts into the rails.)/E(Version 0 was a foamboard mockup of an octagon shaped chassis, employing four vertical thrusters and two horizontal thrusters. Each foamboard panel represented an aluminum T-slot rail. SMS shifted away from this design because of the high density of the T-slot rail and the tolerancing complexity of the 32 required 45\260 angle cuts into the rails.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

328 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 127 0 R/E(Version 1 implemented a set of two enclosure clamps and six 1-in diameter PVC pipes running axially along the chassis tube. The PVC pipes enabled SMS to test the layout prior to cutting into any T-slot rail. The PVC pipes were used as potential mounting locations for other components to the chassis, such as cameras, thruster brackets, manipulator connectors, and leg adapters. Once fit tested and the concept verified, the PVC was replaced for T-slot rails. Version 2 also included a 4 DOF robotic arm, which was not fully implemented in the final design due to its complexity.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

329 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/BlockQuote/P 127 0 R/T(Version 2\220s thruster orientation was determined via an analysis of MATE\220s top ten product demonstration teams. SMS observed that 80% of the top ten teams used a thruster orientation with four vertical thrusters and four horizontal thrusters oriented at a 45\260 angle. SMS then adopted a similar orientation, which provided 6 degrees of freedom. )/E(Version 2\220s thruster orientation was determined via an analysis of MATE\220s top ten product demonstration teams. SMS observed that 80% of the top ten teams used a thruster orientation with four vertical thrusters and four horizontal thrusters oriented at a 45\260 angle. SMS then adopted a similar orientation, which provided 6 degrees of freedom. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

330 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 127 0 R/E(Version 3 introduced enclosure clamps capable of rotating open to provide easy removal of the chassis tube. PVC pipe legs were introduced, providing protection and space to mount the manipulator below the chassis tube. In this design iteration, the manipulator was capable of opening and closing, and was rigidly mounted to a T-slot rail below the chassis. Harpoons were introduced to aid easy pick-up of the basket stars.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

331 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 127 0 R/E(Version 4 added rotation to the manipulator, significantly improving our ability to perform tasks such as installing the power connector or removing biofouling. Two additional cameras were added to improve the pilots\220 field of view, introduced alongside a custom 21-holed anodized aluminum backplate with additional holes to accommodate them. Legs were given a wider stance for additional stability and shortened to locate the manipulator closer to the ground. Bolt locations in the thruster mounts were repositioned to allow easy individual removal of each thruster, increasing the ease of maintenance. Strain relief transitioned from a double-layered polyethylene strap to a steel plate rigidly mounted to the frame of the ROV. Side straps were implemented for ease of handling, allowing the deck team to efficiently remove the ROV from the water. Harpoons were refined to allow pick-up of all rope-based props, extending their functionality beyond the basket-stars. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

332 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 128 0 R/T(Control & Electronics Systems)/E(Control & Electronics Systems)/Pg 18 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

333 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 128 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1375 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

334 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 128 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1376 0 R 0]/K[2]>>
endobj

335 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 128 0 R/E(Figure 14: Eight thrusters and ESCs connected to the PWM Expansion Board)/Pg 18 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

336 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 128 0 R/E(Figure 15: The internal electronics of the ROV connected to the WiFi router)/Pg 18 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

337 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 128 0 R/E(The inner chassis of the ROV is separated by a base plate to enable more mounting space for electronics. To ensure complete electrical isolation of power electronics, the base plate is wrapped in electrical tape to ensure non-conductivity, preventing electrical mishaps before they occur. The layout of connectors on the back plate is strategically placed so that power electronics are compartmentalized into the bottom of the chassis for ease of maintenance, while more sensitive electronics are placed on the top for ease of visual confirmation of functionality. The power-carrying connections are routed through the bottom of the back plate to attach to the 48V-12V buck converter. From there, 12V power is sent to the power distribution network, powering all ESCs, automotive back-up cameras, and the 12V-5V buck converter. On the top half of the chassis, all ESCs are taped together via electrical and cloth tape to aid in ease of component and cable management. Fitting the bulky ESC cables in the chassis is accomplished by routing them through the top section of the chassis rather than into the bottom, avoiding downward strain on joints. Alongside the ESCs, 12V power is stepped down to 5V via a 12V-5V buck converter to feed the Raspberry Pi 4B using a USB-C cable. From the Raspberry Pi 4B, the absolute orientation IMU and PWM expansion board sit on each side of the Raspberry Pi. All three of these boards are secured with robust double sided tape, which is more serviceable and less cumbersome than fastening screws each time to secure components. Short jumper wires were chosen to reduce excess cable space, reducing risk of accidentally pulling a cable out of socket, while also utilizing limited internal space more efficiently.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

338 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 128 0 R/T(Electronic Design & Cabling)/E(Electronic Design & Cabling)/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

339 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 128 0 R/T(Control System Software)/E(Control System Software)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

340 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 128 0 R/E(The control system design that is in place allows for quick implementation for new items that need to be added to the ROV. To control the ROV, the surface side computer sends data packets of the current state of each controller. Based on those packets the Raspberry Pi 4B receives from the surface side computer, the inputs will be run through a controller class that maps all inputs to different thrusters and function of the ROV. The control system requirements were planned out by using a UML class diagram that displays all of the areas that are needed to be covered. Each table of the UML class diagram has its own subsections that go more in depth on how the code will be structured. SMS\220 Auto Leveling System \(ALS\) is utilized to stabilize the ROV in a pilot-defined, custom orientation. This system frees up the pilots mentally and physically to focus on the core aspects of each task. During implementation, the code was maintained in separate branches in GitHub to separate different versions of each function.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

341 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 128 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1377 0 R 0]/K[9]>>
endobj

342 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 129 0 R/T(Control & Electronics Systems)/E(Control & Electronics Systems)/Pg 19 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

343 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 129 0 R/Pg 19 0 R/A[1378 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

344 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 129 0 R/Pg 19 0 R/A[1379 0 R 0]/K[2]>>
endobj

345 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 129 0 R/T(Control Station)/E(Control Station)/Pg 19 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

346 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(The control station set up was designed based on what the company observed during the MATE Regional Qualifier in Watsonville, CA. The team was able to devise that having two surface side computers would be the most effective option during our runs so that pilots wouldn\220t need to be disturbed between tasks to process anything. For ROV controls, SOL-R 2 HOSAS SPACE SIM DUO ThrustMaster controllers are used to provide precise maneuverability to the pilot, and an Xbox One controller for the co-pilot to allow quick and efficient control of different ROV peripherals. For communications, a WiFi router takes the ethernet cable from the tether, and allow the surface side computers to connect via LAN or WiFi connection to the router. To power the ROV, a 48V DC power supply will be utilized to provide the optimal amount of power to the ROV from the surface. There is an easy access kill switch that will shut off all power to the ROV in the case something goes wrong.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

347 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 129 0 R/T(Software Development Process)/E(Software Development Process)/Pg 19 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

348 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(For the software development process, GitHub and Jira were utilized. Jira is a ticket system that can help manage code quality and progression. The process is to have a team member submit their code for review in Jira. Next, a team lead goes in for review, and checks for any issues in the script. If one is found, the script is rejected, and returned to the team member with notes on issues to correct. If the script is approved, it is sent to another team lead for a secondary review. Once the script passes the second check, it will be uploaded to the proper branch in GitHub. After a branch has reached its final version, it\220s added and merged with the code in the main branch. This process upholds a cleanliness standard for all code uploaded to the ROV.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

349 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(Figure 16: UML Class Diagram overview that )/Pg 19 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

350 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(shows each subsection of the control system design)/Pg 19 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

351 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(Figure 17: UML Class Diagram for the Power Control Interface subsection)/Pg 19 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

352 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 129 0 R/Pg 19 0 R/A[1377 0 R 0]/K[10]>>
endobj

353 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(ROV Chassis and Propulsion)/Pg 20 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

354 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 130 0 R/Pg 20 0 R/A[1380 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

355 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(Buoyancy & Ballast)/Pg 20 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

356 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(Propulsion)/Pg 20 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

357 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(The propulsion system used eight Blue Robotics T-200 thrusters, each of which is capable of producing approximately 50 N of forward thrust. To provide stable vertical control, horizontal translation, and rotational motion, four horizontal thruster were mounted around the mid-section of the pressure hull using custom angled mounts positioned at 135\260 from the chassis faces. Since the ROV was intentionally designed to be slightly positively buoyant, the vertical thrusters were oriented so the stronger forward thrust direction of the T200 opposed the buoyant force, improving depth control efficiency and reducing continuous motor strain. The eight thruster configuration was selected to provide greater stability, control authority, and payload handling capability during mission tasks, and although using additional thrusters increased power consumption, system weight, and overall cost, the improved maneuverability and vehicle stability were considered necessary for the tasks requirements.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

358 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(The ROV pressure hull was naturally buoyant. This was countered by the weight of all of the components both inside and out. The team then added some polyethylene foam ballast to be able to get closer to neutral buoyancy. This resulted in a slightly positive buoyancy.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

359 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(feed to achieve the photogrammetry and crab analysis tasks. Initially cameras were designed and connected through use of USB connection from computer to ROV. However, in order to reduce costs the team decided to utilize a streaming protocol starting with MediaMTX. Sending video packets over a network using TCP packets. This resulted in video but at a large latency of nearly 5 seconds, looking into other options the team moved to ffmpeg and got the latency down to around a second after many setting changes. There were many issues with ffmpeg on top of the minor latency, and the team struggled to establish connection and difficulty integrating it into the GUI. )/Pg 20 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

360 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(Later on, the switch was made to using Gstreamer to see if we can achieve better results with it than MediaMTX. Upon receiving the feed from the cameras, the results showed that there was a latency of 0.1 seconds, which is vastly superior to the latency from MediaMTX. The process to start up the cameras was much quicker due to its pipeline commands being easily readable. Gstreamer is also very compatible with QT, making the process to join the two more convenient. )/Pg 20 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

361 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(The sensors that were selected were the Adafruit BNO085 9-DOF IMU and the Blue Robotics Bar02 Depth Sensor. The IMU helped with on the spot level)/Pg 20 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

362 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(correction, and keeps the ROV perfectly oriented when doing autonomous movement. The Bar02 Depth Sensor performs the measurements in the water, and outputs the depth in water. )/Pg 20 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

363 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(VOX CAM7s, which are traditionally automotive back-up cameras, were used for the front, back, and bottom cameras. The AUTO VOX CAM7 cameras received a water proof rating of IP69K, so they are able to perform well under water without sustaining water damage.  When these cameras were tested with the 3DF Zephyr, the photogrammetry software being utilized, the 3D models would not come out looking like the desired object. To solve this issue, a DeepWater Exploration exploreHD USB Camera from Blue Robotics was used to provide a clear, clean video  )/Pg 20 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

364 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(Figure 18: AUTO VOX CAM7 on the ROV)/Pg 20 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

365 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E(Payload and Tools)/Pg 20 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

366 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 130 0 R/Pg 20 0 R/A[1381 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
