413 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(This year, Sequoia Marine Solutions has designed the Aquatic Data probe for Autonomous Monitoring \(A.D.A.M.\). A.D.A.M. utilizes a peristaltic pump as a buoyancy engine, to facilitate vertical profiling by changing the density of the float with water intake and output to descend and ascend respectively. Extensive testing of the peristaltic pump determined that while the flow rate had a higher variance in colder temperatures, the flow rate remained relatively constant over time regardless, including cold-start and kink-tube scenarios. )/Pg 24 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

414 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(In addition, A.D.A.M. features a stainless steel internal rail system that allows the internal electronics to be easily removed from the float chassis. Two side rails and two shelves \(one vertical, one horizontal\) have been custom machined in-house from stainless steel to fit the float\220s storage needs and are attached to the topmost endcap for ease of access and removal as well as safety \(so that the weight of the railing system does not push out the bottom endcap\). Customized ballast plates \(also machined in-house from stainless steel\) allow for adjustments in increments of ~ 4%, 2%, and 0.5% of the overall mass when necessary. The top endcap was additionally machined to hold an NPT-threaded hole for water intake and a pressure release.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

415 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(A.D.A.M. utilizes a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 microcontroller, which communicates with peripheral devices and processes all data collected during each vertical profile. The Pi Pico 2 connects to a pressure/depth sensor via one of two built in I2C buses, and uses this data to calculate subsurface position prior to wireless transmission. A.D.A.M. is designed to execute a PID loop, in which it repeatedly performs a vertical profile while also following a stabilization algorithm to calculate the required pump input or output to account for buoyancy changes and depth corrections. All data collected during vertical profiling is transmitted via LoRA using 915 MHz frequencies and an internal antenna, offering a secure and power efficient method for wireless communications underwater due to lower signal absorption. Power is supplied by a 2000 mA/hr NiMH AA rechargeable battery pack, ensuring sufficient power for ~1hr underwater operation, alongside a 2A blade fuse providing sufficient overcurrent protection for the 1.65A FLA.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

